Compensatory Biomechanical Pain Syndrome
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Pain secondary to altered biomechanics, Adjacent to prior injury site, Asymmetrical gait/posture, Myofascial overload

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-014
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe pain crisis in the "good" leg preventing any weight-bearing, following weeks of compensating for a contralateral injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient who recently suffered a left knee injury, now presenting with sudden, severe, and functionally limiting pain in the right hip and lumbar spine. Urgent physical assessment was necessary to differentiate between a new acute structural injury, deep vein thrombosis in the overloaded limb, or severe compensatory myofascial failure, and to implement immediate mechanical correction and analgesia prior to standard clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've been hobbling on a bad left knee for three weeks, and now my right hip and back have completely seized up; I can't take a single step."
History of Present Illness: Patient recovering from a left medial collateral ligament (MCL) sprain presents with insidious onset of right hip and right-sided lumbar pain that acutely worsened today. Timeline: Developed an asymmetrical, antalgic gait 3 weeks ago to offload the left knee; right-sided ache began 10 days ago, worsening significantly toward the end of each day. Today, after an extended period of standing, the right hip and paraspinal muscles went into a severe, acute spasm. Reports a deep, burning fatigue in the right gluteal region.
Functional Status: Totally incapacitated by pain in the compensatory limb; unable to bear weight bilaterally now.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk due to bilateral lower limb mechanical failure (primary injury left, severe compensatory failure right).

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 136/84 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, frustrated, resting in a lateral decubitus position, avoiding any weight on the right pelvis.
Physical Exam: Left knee shows resolving mild MCL laxity with a bulky, heavy hinged brace in situ. Right lower limb and pelvis exam reveals profound myofascial trigger points with taut bands in the right gluteus medius, tensor fasciae latae, and right lumbar quadratus lumborum. Extreme local twitch responses and radiating pain upon palpation of these right-sided structures. Right hip joint range of motion is physically restricted by muscle spasm, but fundamentally painless at the joint capsule level. Observation of standing posture shows a pronounced rightward pelvic tilt and functional scoliosis. No calf swelling, negative Homan's sign bilaterally.
Environmental Observations: Walking stick present but clearly of incorrect height for the patient, exacerbating the asymmetrical gait.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular paracetamol for the left knee, which is ineffective for the new right-sided severe muscle spasms.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Compensatory Biomechanical Pain Syndrome (Right Hip/Lumbar Myofascial Overload) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis (Right Leg) - Ruled out by absence of distal swelling, normal calf palpation, and the clear myofascial nature of the proximal pain.

Right Hip Osteoarthritis Flare - Ruled out by normal internal/external rotation of the hip joint itself and the specific focal muscular trigger points mimicking joint pain.

Lumbar Radiculopathy - Ruled out by normal lower limb neurovascular exam, negative straight leg raise, and pain remaining localized above the knee.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, immobilizing pain crisis driven entirely by the biomechanical failure of compensatory musculature. The primary left knee injury forced a prolonged, uncorrected antalgic gait, leading to acute myofascial overload of the contralateral hip and spine. The patient had reached a point of bilateral functional failure, posing a severe fall risk and preventing basic ADLs. Postponing evaluation would have left the patient stranded and in severe distress. Urgent after-hours assessment safely excluded DVT and primary joint pathology in the "good" leg, identifying the mechanical root cause. This allowed for the immediate provision of muscle relaxants, adjustment of mobility aids to restore kinetic balance, and education to prevent further functional decline.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Diazepam 2mg BD PRN for 3 days to break the severe compensatory muscle spasms. Applied topical NSAID gel (Diclofenac) to the right gluteal and lumbar regions.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Readjust the walking stick to the correct height and strictly use it in the hand opposite the original injured knee. Consequence: Immediate restoration of a symmetrical gait pattern, offloading the overworked right hip and stopping the severe muscle cramps (negative reinforcement).
Action: Perform targeted gluteal stretching (figure-four stretch) while seated. Consequence: Release of the taut muscular bands, providing a sudden, deep sense of mechanical relief and improved mobility (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for immediate gait retraining and biomechanical correction.
DME/Safety (Optional): Corrected the height of the existing walking stick.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to coordinate integrated rehabilitation for both the primary injury and the compensatory syndrome.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden swelling or deep aching in either calf, shortness of breath, or new neurological symptoms like numbness or weakness in the legs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of mechanical overload. Resolution depends entirely on correcting the abnormal walking pattern. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute muscle spasms will settle quickly with medication and rest, but the pain will immediately return if you continue to limp heavily and overload the "good" side.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your calf suddenly swells up, if you develop chest pain or trouble breathing, or if you lose feeling in your legs.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have literally exhausted the muscles on your "good" side by forcing them to do the work of both legs for three weeks. The Valium will force those exhausted, cramped muscles to relax, while adjusting your walking stick will stop you from overloading them tomorrow.
Actionable Steps: Stop limping without support. Use the adjusted walking stick strictly. The goal is a smooth, even walking rhythm, even if it is slow. Do not try to "power through" on your good leg.

